WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
David: Welcome to LitShare.

2
00:00:02.525 --> 00:00:05.150
This is Protein Degradation English
Corner.

3
00:00:05.212 --> 00:00:06.462
Episode six.

4
00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.650
Science, Gabriel C. Lander.

5
00:00:10.375 --> 00:00:12.950
How Molecular Glues Close Cereblon.

6
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:17.325
Today we ask a simple question with a
surprising answer.

7
00:00:18.225 --> 00:00:23.775
Molecular glue drugs work by sticking a
protein-degrading machine to a new target.

8
00:00:24.625 --> 00:00:27.975
But how does a tiny drug reshape the
machine itself?

9
00:00:28.875 --> 00:00:33.275
Alex: The machine is cereblon, C-R-B-N for
short.

10
00:00:33.575 --> 00:00:38.925
It is the substrate receptor of a
ubiquitin ligase called C-R-L-4.

11
00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:47.375
Its job is to pick up proteins and hand
them to the ligase, which tags them with
ubiquitin, marking them for destruction.

12
00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:59.000
And cereblon is also the target of a
family of drugs called C-E-L-MoD agents,
short for cereblon E3 ligase modulatory
drugs.

13
00:01:00.825 --> 00:01:01.225
Lenalidomide is the famous one.

14
00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:07.275
It has been a first-line treatment for
multiple myeloma for more than a decade.

15
00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:15.200
David: So the drug binds cereblon, and
cereblon grabs a new protein it would
normally ignore.

16
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:16.475
Alex: Exactly.

17
00:01:17.425 --> 00:01:24.125
The drug sits in a pocket on cereblon
called the thalidomide-binding domain, and
it creates a new surface.

18
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:34.800
A protein that would never bind cereblon
on its own, a so-called neosubstrate, now
sticks to that surface, gets tagged, and
gets destroyed.

19
00:01:35.575 --> 00:01:37.675
That is the molecular glue idea.

20
00:01:37.675 --> 00:01:53.875
David: And this paper, from Gabriel
Lander's group at Scripps Research and
Bristol-Myers Squibb, asks what the drug
actually does to cereblon — not just where
it binds, but how it changes the shape of
the whole complex.

21
00:01:54.500 --> 00:02:04.400
Alex: To answer that, they used cryo-
electron microscopy, cryo-E-M for short,
to look at cereblon in solution, with no
drug at all.

22
00:02:05.075 --> 00:02:13.200
Cereblon works together with an adaptor
protein called D-D-B-1, DNA damage-binding
protein one.

23
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.200
And here is the surprise.

24
00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:18.425
Without a drug, cereblon is open.

25
00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:19.550
David: Open?

26
00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:23.150
I thought every structure of cereblon
showed it closed.

27
00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.175
Alex: Every crystal structure did.

28
00:02:26.625 --> 00:02:35.125
Over a dozen of them show the two ends of
cereblon, the Lon domain and the
thalidomide-binding domain, pressed
tightly together.

29
00:02:35.825 --> 00:02:37.275
That is the closed form.

30
00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:50.225
But in solution, with no drug and no
substrate, the new cryo-E-M structure
shows something else: the two domains
separated, at roughly a forty-five-degree
angle.

31
00:02:50.875 --> 00:02:54.950
The protein is open, in every single
particle they looked at.

32
00:02:55.375 --> 00:03:01.000
David: So the closed form drug designers
have studied for years is not the resting
state at all.

33
00:03:01.675 --> 00:03:02.075
Alex: That is the point.

34
00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.275
And there is a reason it stays open.

35
00:03:05.925 --> 00:03:18.200
A flexible loop called the sensor loop
reaches across and holds on to the middle
of the protein and to D-D-B-1, acting like
a tether that keeps the two domains apart.

36
00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.850
David: Then what happens when a drug
arrives?

37
00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:23.800
Alex: They tried pomalidomide first.

38
00:03:24.475 --> 00:03:26.025
The drug binds, no question.

39
00:03:26.575 --> 00:03:29.950
They can see it sitting in its pocket,
even in the open form.

40
00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:32.025
But binding is not enough.

41
00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:35.600
Only about twenty percent of the particles
close up.

42
00:03:36.225 --> 00:03:40.875
The rest stay open, with the drug bound
and the sensor loop still tethered.

43
00:03:41.275 --> 00:03:44.475
David: So binding and closing are two
separate steps.

44
00:03:45.325 --> 00:03:48.500
The drug can sit in the pocket without
flipping the switch.

45
00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:49.750
Alex: Exactly.

46
00:03:50.575 --> 00:03:55.450
And that matters, because only the closed
form can grab a substrate.

47
00:03:56.150 --> 00:04:05.525
To close, the sensor loop has to let go of
its tether, fold into a tight beta
hairpin, and tuck itself between the two
domains.

48
00:04:05.525 --> 00:04:21.050
Then a stretch at the very start of
cereblon, residues forty-eight to sixty-
three, becomes ordered and wraps around
the thalidomide-binding domain like a
belt, holding the closed form together.

49
00:04:21.425 --> 00:04:23.375
David: Did they test that belt?

50
00:04:24.125 --> 00:04:24.650
Alex: They did.

51
00:04:25.075 --> 00:04:34.100
They cut off the first sixty-three amino
acids, and with the belt gone,
pomalidomide could barely close cereblon
at all.

52
00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:37.100
Less than two percent of the particles
closed.

53
00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:39.025
The belt is essential.

54
00:04:39.025 --> 00:04:42.200
David: So pomalidomide is a weak closer.

55
00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:44.225
What about the newer drugs?

56
00:04:45.125 --> 00:04:46.875
Alex: That is where it gets interesting.

57
00:04:48.500 --> 00:05:01.275
Iberdomide, a next-generation C-E-L-MoD
drug, binds cereblon about twenty times
more tightly than pomalidomide, and it
degrades the protein Ikaros about twenty-
four times better.

58
00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:06.925
In the cryo-E-M data, nearly fifty percent
of the particles are closed.

59
00:05:08.475 --> 00:05:21.275
Iberdomide carries extra groups, a phenyl
ring and a morpholino group, that arch
over the sensor loop, helping it fold into
its beta hairpin shape and pushing the
protein toward the closed form.

60
00:05:21.550 --> 00:05:23.900
David: Is there independent evidence for
that?

61
00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:28.000
Cryo-E-M is one technique.

62
00:05:29.175 --> 00:05:29.475
Alex: Yes.

63
00:05:29.950 --> 00:05:38.625
They also used hydrogen-deuterium exchange
mass spectrometry, which measures how
exposed each part of a protein is to
water.

64
00:05:39.275 --> 00:05:47.775
With iberdomide bound, the interface
between the two domains becomes much less
exposed, exactly as closing predicts.

65
00:05:47.775 --> 00:05:54.575
The biggest change is in the sensor loop
itself, consistent with it folding into
that buried hairpin.

66
00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:56.900
David: And the substrate?

67
00:05:57.775 --> 00:05:59.800
When does Ikaros actually join?

68
00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:01.575
Alex: Only after closing.

69
00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:09.100
They mixed cereblon with Ikaros, using its
first three zinc finger domains, and added
either pomalidomide or iberdomide.

70
00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:14.075
In every case, Ikaros shows up only on the
closed form.

71
00:06:14.675 --> 00:06:16.975
They never saw it bound to the open form.

72
00:06:17.625 --> 00:06:19.075
The order of events is clear.

73
00:06:19.075 --> 00:06:24.650
The drug binds, the protein closes, and
only then does the substrate get
recruited.

74
00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:27.775
David: There is another moving part here,
right?

75
00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:30.900
D-D-B-1 itself has a mobile piece.

76
00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:32.525
Alex: Yes.

77
00:06:33.250 --> 00:06:42.650
One of the three propeller-shaped domains
of D-D-B-1, called B-P-B, can sit in three
distinct positions: linear, hinged, and
twisted.

78
00:06:43.325 --> 00:06:51.950
In the free complex, about sixty-five
percent are linear, twenty to thirty
percent are hinged, and under ten percent
are twisted.

79
00:06:51.950 --> 00:07:02.175
The twisted position is the one that
dominates when the ligase core is
attached, so this may be how the complex
positions the substrate for tagging.

80
00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:04.025
David: Now the star of the paper:
mezigdomide.

81
00:07:06.275 --> 00:07:13.825
Alex: Mezigdomide is the newest of the
three, and the most effective in patients
who no longer respond to lenalidomide or
pomalidomide.

82
00:07:15.325 --> 00:07:19.700
With mezigdomide, one hundred percent of
the particles are closed.

83
00:07:20.175 --> 00:07:21.500
David: What makes it so different?

84
00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:23.300
Alex: It reaches further.

85
00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:34.925
The drug has a benzonitrile group that
sticks out of the binding pocket and
reaches across to the Lon domain, where it
is held by two phenylalanine side chains,
at positions one-oh-two and one-five-oh.

86
00:07:38.275 --> 00:07:45.225
So the drug itself staples the two domains
together, doing the job the N-terminal
belt normally does.

87
00:07:46.075 --> 00:07:52.500
In fact, in the mezigdomide data, many
closed particles do not even show the
belt.

88
00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:54.500
The drug replaces it.

89
00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:58.125
David: And does that translate into better
degradation?

90
00:07:59.100 --> 00:07:59.450
Alex: It does.

91
00:08:00.825 --> 00:08:13.800
Ikaros is recruited to the mezigdomide-
bound closed form, and in test-tube
ubiquitination assays with the full ligase
core, Ikaros gets tagged more efficiently
than with the older drugs.

92
00:08:14.425 --> 00:08:24.425
The authors' model is that mezigdomide
keeps the protein closed longer, so each
round of substrate binding and tagging
runs more smoothly.

93
00:08:25.025 --> 00:08:28.750
David: So the design lesson is not just
about binding tightly.

94
00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:30.425
Alex: That is the takeaway.

95
00:08:31.175 --> 00:08:38.525
For years, cereblon drug design focused on
affinity: how tightly the drug binds, and
how tightly the substrate binds.

96
00:08:39.250 --> 00:08:46.550
This paper adds a third dimension: how
well the drug closes cereblon, and how
long it keeps it closed.

97
00:08:47.525 --> 00:08:55.850
A drug that staples the closed form, or
stabilizes the sensor loop hairpin, can
outperform one that merely binds well.

98
00:08:56.700 --> 00:09:02.650
The authors suggest the same idea could
apply to other molecular glues, and even
to PROTACs.

99
00:09:03.325 --> 00:09:05.200
David: What are the limits here?

100
00:09:05.775 --> 00:09:06.150
Alex: A few.

101
00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:13.700
These are snapshots of purified proteins
in a test tube, not movies inside a living
cell.

102
00:09:14.725 --> 00:09:22.950
The iberdomide-sensor loop interaction is
probably dynamic, and its details sit at
the edge of what the maps can resolve.

103
00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:34.975
And stapling the closed form has not yet
been tested on other glues or PROTACs — it
is a proposal, backed by three drugs, not
a proven rule.

104
00:09:34.975 --> 00:09:37.775
David: Still, it reframes the whole
problem.

105
00:09:38.225 --> 00:09:39.625
The drug is not just a glue.

106
00:09:40.425 --> 00:09:41.025
It is a switch.

107
00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:42.300
Alex: Exactly.

108
00:09:43.325 --> 00:09:45.075
Cereblon sits open, waiting.

109
00:09:45.850 --> 00:09:52.650
The drug binds, the sensor loop refolds,
the belt fastens, and the machine closes
around its new target.

110
00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:57.875
That closing step, not just the binding,
is what makes the drug work.

111
00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:00.125
David: That is it for this episode.

112
00:10:00.650 --> 00:10:04.975
You have been listening to Protein
Degradation English Corner, from Litshare.

113
00:10:05.575 --> 00:10:06.425
See you next time.
